The javadoc clarifies the expected behavior:
- If chars is not null, the method chooses characters from positions between start and end in the chars array.
- It expects chars to be at least (end - start) + 1 characters long.
- It throws ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException if this is not the case.
- If start and end are both 0, they are set depending on letters/numbers flags: for chars != null, start and end still dictate the positions in chars.

Therefore:
- Passing start=0 and end=0 means an expected range of length 1 in chars (characters at index 0)
- But in the fixed code, when both start=0 and end=0, end is overridden to Integer.MAX_VALUE if letters/numbers are false (our test case). This is incorrect when chars != null.
- Because this updated end creates a huge range (gap), the random index selection goes out of chars array bounds.

This implies the bug is in these lines (231-238):
```java
if (start == 0 && end == 0) {
    if (!letters && !numbers) {
        end = Integer.MAX_VALUE;
    } else {
        end = 'z' + 1;
        start = ' ';                
    }
}
```
These should only be applied when chars == null (i.e., when selecting from char code points). Otherwise, when chars is supplied, start and end indices should not be reset like that.

# Conclusion
Resetting start and end when they are both 0 should be conditional on chars == null. Otherwise, passing start=0 and end=0 with a small chars array will cause ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException because the gap becomes huge and indexes out of chars array bounds.

This fully explains the failure of the test and matches the current bug report.

